# Enhanced runtime-calibration state

## Goal

Expose the APC runtime-calibration state as a read-only Home Assistant enum
sensor so users can see when a UPS is calibrating its battery runtime.

## Scope

- Apply to Smart-UPS and SMT/SMX/SRT UPS families only; do not create the
  entity for Rack PDUs.
- Add `upsAdvTestCalibrationResults` to the existing regular self-test SNMP
  polling set:
  `1.3.6.1.4.1.318.1.1.1.7.2.6.0`.
- Reuse the existing executor-backed SNMP helper, OID deduplication, and
  coordinator merge path. Do not create a separate poll, configuration option,
  write control, or dependency.

## Entity

- Coordinator and register key: `snmp_runtime_calibration_status`.
- Entity name: `Runtime Calibration Status`.
- Model it as an enum sensor with a stable config-entry-based unique ID and
  enable it by default for supported UPS families.
- Decode the integer result as:

  | Value | State |
  | ---: | --- |
  | 1 | Calibration Complete |
  | 2 | Cannot Calibrate — Battery Not Fully Charged |
  | 3 | Calibration In Progress |
  | 4 | Calibration Refused |
  | 5 | Calibration Aborted |
  | 6 | Calibration Pending |

- Preserve an unknown numeric value as `Unknown (<value>)`.
- If the OID is absent, invalid, or unavailable, retain the entity and report
  it unavailable. It must not interrupt Modbus data, other SNMP telemetry, or
  setup.

## Boundaries

- This is status telemetry only. Do not expose the calibration command OID or
  add a button that can start or cancel calibration.
- Do not alter the existing self-test sensors, polling cadence, sensor
  availability rules, energy rollover tracking, or release metadata.

## Tests and acceptance

- Extend the existing self-test helper tests to assert that the calibration OID
  is requested with the existing five OIDs, parsed as an integer, and remains
  independently unavailable on an OID failure.
- Test all six enum labels and an unknown code.
- Test supported UPS setup creates the default-enabled entity with its stable
  unique ID; Rack PDU setup must not create it.
- Run `./.venv/bin/pytest -q` and `make lint`.
